  <section id="memo" aria-labelledby="memo-h">
    <h2 id="memo-h">Decision memo</h2>

    <p>
      The memo asks one question: whether a p/Z x-intercept may be used as the gas-in-place
      basis for a depletion or development decision, when the only evidence for the
      volumetric assumption is the quality of the straight line.
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        <strong>Recommendation, conditional: do not adopt a p/Z x-intercept as a volumetric
        gas-in-place basis until the drive mechanism has been established from an
        observation other than the p/Z line itself.</strong>
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      On the evidence of this study, the fit statistic and the chronological reconstruction
      error are both incapable of distinguishing a closed tank from an aquifer-supported
      reservoir whose gas in place is overstated by {base.errorPct.toFixed(1)} percent and
      whose remaining gas in place is overstated by {base.remainingErrorPct.toFixed(1)}
      percent. The recommendation is not "p/Z is unreliable": the method is exact under its
      stated assumptions, and the finding is about the conditions under which a straight line
      stops carrying the information people read from it.
    </p>

    <h3>The mechanism</h3>
    <p>
      It is a pore-volume bookkeeping error, not a statistical one. The volumetric balance
      assumes the gas occupies a fixed hydrocarbon pore volume. When water encroaches, part
      of that pore volume is no longer available to gas, pressure is held up relative to the
      volumetric expectation, the p/Z line is flatter than it should be, and a flatter line
      has its x-intercept further to the right. The regression absorbs the gentle curvature
      into the slope, which is precisely the parameter that sets the x-intercept. R-squared
      measures the part the regression could not absorb, so it cannot report the part it
      did.
    </p>

    <h3>The required extra evidence</h3>
    <p>
      A <strong>metered water-production record</strong>. That is the new observation. A Cole
      or pot-aquifer plot is not: both are re-plots of the same pressure and production
      history the p/Z fit already uses, so they add information only to the extent that
      produced water is separately measured and entered into them. Neither plot was run in
      this study. The conversion from a water record to an influx is two steps, not one:
      cumulative produced water W_p is a standard-condition volume, its reservoir-volume
      equivalent is W_p·B_w, and total influx W_e follows only through an aquifer model or a
      closed balance.
    </p>

    <h3>Non-uniqueness is practical, not structural</h3>
    <p>
      The estimator fits two parameters — a free intercept and a free slope — and G = −a/b is
      derived from both. Adding an influx term gives a two-parameter physical fit in (G, J)
      that is identifiable in principle on noise-free data: a direct least-squares
      minimisation over (G, J) against the base-case history, with no influx observation
      supplied at all, returns G within 1.0e-11 relative of the truth. What defeats it in the
      field is conditioning. Profiling the noise-free base history over J at fixed G gives a
      best-fit misfit of about 20.3 psia of p/Z at the +12.5 percent in G the volumetric fit
      actually returns — inside the 10-to-50-psi band this study assumes — so at field
      accuracy the aquifer parameter absorbs a large error in G at no detectable cost. The
      honest statement is practical, noise-limited ambiguity, and the reason to measure water
      is that it breaks the conditioning.
    </p>
    <p class="provenance">
      Provenance: that joint fit and that profile were computed during a claims-audit
      correction pass by calling the committed generator directly. Neither is pre-registered,
      neither is in the shipped run summary, and neither is a gated result.
    </p>

    <h3>The methodological lesson</h3>
    <p>
      A fit statistic is a test of the residuals. A conditional holdout reconstruction is a
      test of the reconstructed series over the horizon it covers. Neither is a test of an
      extrapolated quantity that lives outside the data, neither is a physical interpretation
      of the drive mechanism, and neither establishes that a number is adequate to carry a
      development decision. A gas-in-place estimate from an x-intercept should be quoted with
      the observed depletion fraction attached — here
      {" "}{(base.depletionFractionObserved * 100).toFixed(1)} percent — should be labelled as
      inventory in place rather than as a recoverable volume or a reserve, and should never
      be quoted with a confidence interval as its only statement of uncertainty.
    </p>
  </section>

